In addition to geometric stress concentrations and material selection, the mean stress of the applied loading, expressed by the stress ratio  R , play a key role in the assessment of fatigue strength. For unwelded components, the FKM guideline [ 6 ], based on the work of Schütz [ 7 ], introduces the mean stress sensitivity M to account for the effect of mean stress on allowable stress amplitudes. As ductility and creep capacity of the material decrease, the sensitivity to mean stress increases accordingly. However, current fatigue design codes for welded components generally neglect the influence of applied mean stress [ 8 ], assuming that welding-induced residual stresses are close to the material’s yield strength [ 9 ]. In addition, studies by Fisher et al. [ 10 ,  11 ] have shown that, in large welded structures made of mild steel, fatigue life remains largely unaffected by the applied mean stress within a stress ratio range of  R = - 0.6 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
