The second material studied was the fully austenitic, stainless, and nickel-free TWIP (twinning-induced plasticity) steel X30MnCrN16-14 (material number 1.4678), classified under DIN prEN 10088-2 [ 24 ]. Due to the instability of austenite at room temperature in low-alloy steels, achieving a fully austenitic microstructure requires high levels of alloying elements, especially manganese, as used in TWIP steels. These steels are characterized by a low stacking fault energy, which promotes the formation of mechanical twins during plastic deformation. The twinning mechanism significantly hinders dislocation movement and results in pronounced work hardening, combining very high strength with exceptional ductility. For tube production, the steel was solution-annealed and subsequently temper-rolled to increase the yield strength to at least  R p , 0.2 = 800 M P a \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
