The crack initiation and propagation behavior differed considerably between the two steels. In the fine-grain structural steel specimens, cracks initiated in regions of high stress concentration and grew into areas of lower stress until a stress equilibrium was reached or, in some cases, until the testing machine’s failure criterion was met. This behavior is likely attributable to significant residual and assembly induced stresses. In contrast, the austenitic steel specimens exhibited unexpected behavior. Despite being subjected entirely to compressive loading, they followed a continuous failure pattern at load levels comparable to those of the fine-grain structural steel. Approximately half of the specimens showed weld-toe cracking, while the remainder exhibited root failure. This behavior resulted in a free SN curve exponent of  m = 1.9 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
